r them but that wouldn't work for me  we talked about Stephen King in the  previous segment maybe do you have an  imaginary uh reader that you write for  he talked about that and on writing so  people are writing for someone that's  been dead for 15 years and others a  harsh High School teacher and you know  it's usually my wife Melissa okay I want  her to be impressed yeah so I really  write with the idea often of uh she'll  love this you know she'll be impressed  she'll think I'm really smart because  I've written this you know and it's  helpful you know it's uh yeah and then  it's it's just discouraging when I  realize I failed  but she doesn't always react that way to  everything I write but  um you know I learned from that and and  go on to make it better  and is there a particular style of music  you listen to you said while you're  driving you you come up with great oh  well when I'm driving I love to listen  to anything I sing and I just make a  complete fool of myself and we have a  convertible so it's even worse you know  everyone can see me there are kids  pointing at me as they drive away but I  love it I I sing I just get very  involved in the music and I you know I  love the music mostly of my era you know  I love 70s music and and I love the  classics from the 50s and 60s and  Rolling Stones and Rolling Stones and  Bruce Springsteen who still is a force  today of course uh  um I and you know just I love just  emotional kind of stuff that I can get  feelings out when I'm writing I listen  to music also but I listen to attention  to film scores  um and you know lyrics because that  would distract me but that's enormously  helpful to listen to music as I write is  and I've got an enormous collection of  scores that I listen to  so Lou Reed driving down the street and  then and then uh  how does a writer create conflict  oh well that's a great question and it's  an important one because that is really  the central thing about drama I think is  you know co